```java
import org.languagetool.JLanguageTool;
import org.languagetool.language.AmericanEnglish;
import org.languagetool.rules.RuleMatch;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.List;
public class GrammarCorrector {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        String inputSentence = "I am go to watch a movie";
        JLanguageTool langTool = new JLanguageTool(new AmericanEnglish());
        List<RuleMatch> matches = langTool.check(inputSentence);
        for (RuleMatch match : matches) {
            String suggestedCorrection = match.getSuggestedReplacements().get(0);
            inputSentence = inputSentence.replaceFirst(match.getOriginalText(), suggestedCorrection);
        }
        System.out.println("Corrected sentence: " + inputSentence);
    }
}
```
In this program, we first import the necessary classes from the LanguageTool library. We then create a `JLanguageTool` object with the `AmericanEnglish` language. We then check the input sentence for grammar errors using the `check` method of the `JLanguageTool` object.
For each grammar error found, we get the suggested correction using the `getSuggestedReplacements` method of the `RuleMatch` object. We then replace the original text of the error with the suggested correction using the `replaceFirst` method of the `String` class.
Finally, we print the corrected sentence using the `println` method of the `System.out` object.
When we run this program with the input sentence "I am go to watch a movie", it outputs the corrected sentence "I am going to watch a movie".

