The Fibonacci sequence can be calculated in PHP using a recursive function or an iterative loop. Here is an example of a recursive function:
```
function fibonacci($n) {
  if ($n == 0) {
    return 0;
  } else if ($n == 1) {
    return 1;
  } else {
    return fibonacci($n-1) + fibonacci($n-2);
  }
}
```
To calculate the nth number in the sequence, you would call the function with the desired value of n:
```
$result = fibonacci(10); // returns 55
```
While this method is simple and easy to understand, it can be slow for large values of n because it involves a lot of redundant calculations.
To improve performance, you can use an iterative loop instead. Here is an example:
```
function fibonacci($n) {
  $a = 0;
  $b = 1;
  for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
    $temp = $a;
    $a = $b;
    $b = $temp + $b;
  }
  return $a;
}
```
This method is faster because it only calculates each number in the sequence once.
There are many other mathematical formulas that can be leveraged in PHP programming to optimize code performance. One example is the Sieve of Eratosthenes, which is used to find all prime numbers up to a given limit. Here is an example of how it can be implemented in PHP:
```
function sieve($limit) {
  $primes = array_fill(0, $limit+1, true);
  $primes[0] = false;
  $primes[1] = false;
  for ($i = 2; $i <= sqrt($limit); $i++) {
    if ($primes[$i]) {
      for ($j = $i*$i; $j <= $limit; $j += $i) {
        $primes[$j] = false;
      }
    }
  }
  return array_keys(array_filter($primes));
}
```
This function returns an array of all prime numbers up to the given limit. It works by initializing an array of boolean values representing whether each number is prime or not, then iterating through the array and marking all multiples of each prime number as not prime.
Another example is the Euclidean algorithm, which is used to find the greatest common divisor of two numbers. Here is an example of how it can be implemented in PHP:
```
function gcd($a, $b) {
  while ($b != 0) {
    $temp = $a % $b;
    $a = $b;
    $b = $temp;
  }
  return $a;
}
```
This function returns the greatest common divisor of $a and $b. It works by repeatedly taking the remainder of $a divided by $b and swapping $a and $b until $b is zero, at which point $a is the greatest common divisor.

