To incorporate real-time collaboration between multiple users with the ability to track changes made by each user in an iOS app, we can leverage Firebase Realtime Database in combination with Swift code. Here are the steps to achieve this:
1. Set up Firebase Realtime Database in your iOS app by following the Firebase documentation.
2. Create a data model that represents the data structure you want to collaborate on. For example, if you want to collaborate on a document, your data model could include properties like title, content, and author.
3. Use Firebase Realtime Database to store and retrieve the data model objects. You can use Firebase's real-time synchronization to ensure that any changes made by one user are immediately reflected in the app for all other users.
4. Implement a real-time collaboration feature that allows multiple users to edit the same data model object simultaneously. You can use Firebase Realtime Database's transactional updates to ensure that changes made by each user are applied in a consistent and conflict-free manner.
5. Implement change tracking by storing a log of all changes made to the data model objects in Firebase Realtime Database. You can use Firebase's event listeners to track changes made by each user and store them in the log.
6. To ensure scalability and efficiency, you can use Firebase Realtime Database's sharding feature to distribute the data across multiple database instances. This will ensure that the app can handle a large number of users collaborating on the same data.
7. To optimize your Swift code, you can use Swift's Codable protocol to serialize and deserialize your data model objects. This will make it easier to store and retrieve the objects from Firebase Realtime Database.
Here is an example of how you can use Swift code to implement real-time collaboration using Firebase Realtime Database:
```swift
// Define a data model for a document
struct Document: Codable {
    var title: String
    var content: String
    var author: String
}
// Get a reference to the Firebase Realtime Database
let database = Database.database().reference()
// Create a new document object
let newDocument = Document(title: "My Document", content: "", author: "John")
// Store the document object in Firebase Realtime Database
database.child("documents").childByAutoId().setValue(newDocument)
// Listen for changes to the document object
database.child("documents").observe(.childChanged) { snapshot in
    // Update the document object in the app
    let updatedDocument = try? snapshot.value(as: Document.self)
}
// Update the document object in Firebase Realtime Database
let documentRef = database.child("documents").child("documentId")
documentRef.runTransactionBlock { currentData in
    if var document = currentData.value as? [String: Any] {
        // Update the document object
        document["content"] = "New content"
        currentData.value = document
    }
    return TransactionResult.success(withValue: currentData)
}
// Store a log of changes made to the document object
let changeLogRef = database.child("changeLog").childByAutoId()
let changeLogEntry = ["documentId": "documentId", "author": "John", "timestamp": Date().timeIntervalSince1970, "changes": ["content": "New content"]]
changeLogRef.setValue(changeLogEntry)
```
In this example, we define a data model for a document and use Firebase Realtime Database to store and retrieve the document objects. We also listen for changes to the document object and store a log of changes made to the object. Finally, we update the document object in Firebase Realtime Database using a transactional update to ensure consistency and conflict resolution.

