There are many sorting algorithms that can be used to efficiently find the top 5 customers with the highest number of orders in the last month. One common algorithm is the quicksort algorithm. Here's an example of how you can implement it in C++:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <algorithm>
struct Customer {
    std::string name;
    int orders;
};
bool compareCustomers(Customer a, Customer b) {
    return a.orders > b.orders;
}
int main() {
    Customer customers[] = {{"Alice", 10}, {"Bob", 5}, {"Charlie", 15}, {"David", 7}, {"Eve", 20}, {"Frank", 12}};
    int n = sizeof(customers) / sizeof(customers[0]);
    std::sort(customers, customers + n, compareCustomers);
    std::cout << "Top 5 customers:\n";
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        std::cout << customers[i].name << " (" << customers[i].orders << " orders)\n";
    }
    return 0;
}
```
To optimize the performance of this algorithm, you can use data structures such as hash tables or binary trees. For example, you can use a hash table to store the customers and their order counts, and then use a priority queue to efficiently find the top 5 customers. Here's an example of how you can implement this:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <queue>
struct Customer {
    std::string name;
    int orders;
    bool operator<(const Customer& other) const {
        return orders < other.orders;
    }
};
int main() {
    std::unordered_map<std::string, int> orderCounts = {{"Alice", 10}, {"Bob", 5}, {"Charlie", 15}, {"David", 7}, {"Eve", 20}, {"Frank", 12}};
    std::priority_queue<Customer> topCustomers;
    for (auto& pair : orderCounts) {
        Customer customer = {pair.first, pair.second};
        topCustomers.push(customer);
        if (topCustomers.size() > 5) {
            topCustomers.pop();
        }
    }
    std::cout << "Top 5 customers:\n";
    while (!topCustomers.empty()) {
        Customer customer = topCustomers.top();
        std::cout << customer.name << " (" << customer.orders << " orders)\n";
        topCustomers.pop();
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, we use an `unordered_map` to store the customers and their order counts, and a `priority_queue` to store the top 5 customers. We iterate over the customers in the `unordered_map`, and for each customer, we create a `Customer` object and push it onto the `priority_queue`. If the `priority_queue` has more than 5 customers, we pop the smallest customer. Finally, we print out the top 5 customers in the `priority_queue`. This approach has a time complexity of O(n log k), where n is the number of customers and k is the number of top customers we want to find.

