Here's an implementation of binary search algorithm in Java to locate the integer value of 8 within the sorted array [2, 3, 6, 7, 8, 10, 12]:
```
public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {
    int low = 0;
    int high = arr.length - 1;
    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = (low + high) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] == target) {
            return mid;
        } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
            low = mid + 1;
        } else {
            high = mid - 1;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
```
To ensure that the algorithm executes as efficiently as possible, you can make the following optimizations:
1. Use the `int mid = low + (high - low) / 2` formula to calculate the middle index instead of `(low + high) / 2`. This prevents integer overflow when the array size is large.
2. Use the `Arrays.binarySearch()` method instead of implementing the algorithm yourself. This method is part of the Java standard library and is optimized for performance.
3. Ensure that the input array is sorted in ascending order before calling the binary search algorithm. If the array is not sorted, you can use the `Arrays.sort()` method to sort it before searching.
Here's an example of using the `Arrays.binarySearch()` method to locate the integer value of 8 within the sorted array [2, 3, 6, 7, 8, 10, 12]:
```
int[] arr = {2, 3, 6, 7, 8, 10, 12};
int index = Arrays.binarySearch(arr, 8);
if (index >= 0) {
    System.out.println("Found at index " + index);
} else {
    System.out.println("Not found");
}
``` 
