\begin{center}
\textbf{\large The Process of a Computer Booting Up and Initializing its BIOS Settings}
\end{center}
\begin{enumerate}
\item The computer's power supply is turned on, which sends electricity to the motherboard.
\item The motherboard sends a signal to the BIOS (Basic Input/Output System) chip.
\item The BIOS chip performs a Power-On Self-Test (POST) to check the hardware components of the computer, such as the RAM, hard drive, keyboard, and mouse, to ensure that they are functioning properly.
\item If there is an error during the POST, the BIOS will issue a series of beeps to indicate the problem.
\item After the POST is completed, the BIOS reads the Boot Configuration Data (BCD) from the boot device, which is usually the hard drive.
\item The BCD contains information about the operating system installed on the computer and the boot loader, which is responsible for loading the operating system into memory.
\item Once the BIOS has loaded the boot loader, the boot loader takes over and loads the operating system into memory.
\item The operating system then initializes the drivers and other software components needed to run the computer.
\end{enumerate}
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{boot_process.png}
\caption{Diagram of the computer boot process}
\end{figure}
In summary, the process of a computer booting up and initializing its BIOS settings involves the motherboard sending a signal to the BIOS chip, which performs a Power-On Self-Test (POST) to check the hardware components, reads the Boot Configuration Data (BCD), and loads the operating system into memory.
\end{document}

One possible algorithm that can be used to find the shortest path for a delivery drone to reach multiple customers in a city with obstacles and no-fly zones, while also ensuring that the drone can only make right-angle turns is the A* algorithm. Here is a pseudocode implementation of the algorithm:
1. Define the start and end points for the drone's journey, as well as the locations of the customers and any obstacles or no-fly zones.
2. Create an empty open set and a closed set. The open set will contain nodes that are candidates for the next step of the path, while the closed set will contain nodes that have already been visited.
3. Add the start point to the open set.
4. While the open set is not empty, do the following:
   a. Choose the node in the open set with the lowest f-score (the sum of the g-score, which is the cost of reaching the node from the start point, and the h-score, which is an estimate of the cost of reaching the end point from the node).
   b. If the chosen node is the end point, return the path that led to it.
   c. Remove the chosen node from the open set and add it to the closed set.
   d. Generate the successors of the chosen node, which are the nodes that can be reached from it by making a right-angle turn and moving one unit in a cardinal direction (north, south, east, or west). For each successor, do the following:
      i. If the successor is in the closed set, skip it.
      ii. If the successor is an obstacle or a no-fly zone, skip it.
      iii. If the successor is not in the open set, add it and set its g-score to the g-score of the chosen node plus the cost of moving from the chosen node to the successor. Set its h-score to the Manhattan distance (the sum of the absolute differences in x and y coordinates) between the successor and the end point.
      iv. If the successor is already in the open set, update its g-score if the cost of reaching it from the chosen node is lower than its current g-score.
5. If the end point is not reached and the open set is empty, return that there is no path.
Here is the pseudocode implementation of the A* algorithm for finding the shortest path for a delivery drone to reach multiple customers in a city with obstacles and no-fly zones, while also ensuring that the drone can only make right-angle turns:
```
function A_star(start, end, customers, obstacles, no_fly_zones):
    open_set = {start}
    closed_set = {}
    g_scores = {start: 0}
    h_scores = {start: manhattan_distance(start, end)}
    parent_nodes = {}

    while open_set:
        current = min(open_set, key=lambda node: g_scores[node] + h_scores[node])

        if current == end:
            path = []
            while current in parent_nodes:
                path.append(current)
                current = parent_nodes[current]
            path.append(start)
            path.reverse()
            return path

        open_set.remove(current)
        closed_set.add(current)

        for direction in [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]:
            successor = (current[0] + direction[0], current[1] + direction[1])

            if successor in closed_set:
                continue

            if successor in obstacles or successor in no_fly_zones:
                continue

            tentative_g_score = g_scores[current] + 1

            if successor not in open_set or tentative_g_score < g_scores[successor]:
                parent_nodes[successor] = current
                g_scores[successor] = tentative_g_score
                h_scores[successor] = manhattan_distance(successor, end)
                if successor not in open_set:
                    open_set.add(successor)

    return None
function manhattan_distance(a, b):
    return abs(a[0] - b[0]) + abs(a[1] - b[1])
```
Note that this implementation assumes that the drone can only move one unit at a time in a cardinal direction. If the drone can move more than one unit at a time, the cost of moving from one node to another should be adjusted accordingly.

