Can you enhance this C++ program to make it even more challenging? The program should prompt the user with a statement, and then allow them to input either "T" for True or "F" for False. However, the program will not display whether the user's classification was correct or incorrect immediately. Instead, the program will only display the final result after the user has classified all statements correctly. 
To make things even more challenging, the statements will be randomly generated and presented to the user in a table format. Each row of the table will have two statements, and the user will have to classify both statements as True or False before moving onto the next row. Only if the user correctly classifies all statements in the table will the program display the final result.
In addition, you need to ensure that the program generates a unique set of statements for each user, making it much harder for anyone to predict the correct classification. You can also add a time limit for each classification and force the user to complete the classification within the allotted time limit. 
Here is an example of what the program could look like:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <ctime>
using namespace std;
int main() {
  string statement1, statement2, answer1, answer2;
  int correct_count = 0;

  srand(time(NULL)); // seed the random number generator

  for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) { // generate 5 rows of statements
    statement1 = "Statement " + to_string(rand() % 100 + 1) + "A";
    statement2 = "Statement " + to_string(rand() % 100 + 1) + "B";

    cout << "Row " << i << ":" << endl;
    cout << statement1 << "\t" << statement2 << endl;
    cout << "Enter T for True or F for False for both statements within 30 seconds: ";
    time_t start_time = time(NULL);
    cin >> answer1 >> answer2;
    time_t end_time = time(NULL);

    if ((answer1 == "T" && answer2 == "T") && (end_time - start_time <= 30)) {
      correct_count++;
    }
  }

  if (correct_count == 5) {
    cout << "Congratulations! You correctly classified all statements." << endl;
  } else {
    cout << "Sorry, you did not correctly classify all statements." << endl;
  }

  return 0;
} 
Can you modify this code to include more rows of statements or even generate statements randomly? Only the most skilled programmers will be able to crack this code.

