To generate a unique four-letter word for a phrase about friendship in C++, you can use a combination of random number generation and string manipulation. Here's an example code snippet that generates a random four-letter word:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <ctime>
#include <string>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
    srand(time(NULL)); // Initialize random seed

    string letters = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz"; // Define the pool of letters to choose from
    string word = "";

    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        int index = rand() % letters.length(); // Generate a random index within the length of the letters string
        word += letters[index]; // Append the randomly selected letter to the word
    }

    cout << "Your unique four-letter word for friendship is: " << word << endl;

    return 0;
}
```
To incorporate an algorithm to verify that the generated word is free of offensive or derogatory language, you can use a list of banned words and check if the generated word matches any of them. Here's an example code snippet that adds a banned words list and checks if the generated word is free of any of them:
