Some additional security features that can be incorporated into the performance review template in Java include:
1. Role-based access control: This feature allows you to restrict access to sensitive employee data to only authorized personnel. You can assign different roles to different users and grant them access to only the information they need to perform their job functions.
2. Encryption: This feature involves encoding sensitive data to prevent unauthorized access. You can use encryption algorithms to scramble employee data before storing it in the database.
3. Audit trail: This feature allows you to track all changes made to employee data. You can log all user activity and monitor any suspicious behavior.
Here's an example code that includes encryption to protect sensitive employee data:
```
import java.security.*;
import javax.crypto.*;
import javax.crypto.spec.*;
public class PerformanceReview {
  private String employeeName;
  private String reviewerName;
  private String reviewComments;
  private byte[] encryptedEmployeeID;
  public PerformanceReview(String employeeName, String reviewerName, String reviewComments, String employeeID, SecretKey key) throws Exception {
    this.employeeName = employeeName;
    this.reviewerName = reviewerName;
    this.reviewComments = reviewComments;
    this.encryptedEmployeeID = encrypt(employeeID, key);
  }
  public String getEmployeeName() {
    return employeeName;
  }
  public String getReviewerName() {
    return reviewerName;
  }
  public String getReviewComments() {
    return reviewComments;
  }
  public String getEmployeeID(SecretKey key) throws Exception {
    return decrypt(encryptedEmployeeID, key);
  }
  private byte[] encrypt(String data, SecretKey key) throws Exception {
    Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/ECB/PKCS5Padding");
    cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, key);
    return cipher.doFinal(data.getBytes());
  }
  private String decrypt(byte[] encryptedData, SecretKey key) throws Exception {
    Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/ECB/PKCS5Padding");
    cipher.init(Cipher.DECRYPT_MODE, key);
    return new String(cipher.doFinal(encryptedData));
  }
}
```
In this example, the `employeeID` field is encrypted using the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) algorithm before it is stored in the `encryptedEmployeeID` field. The encryption key is passed as a parameter to the constructor and the `getEmployeeID` method to ensure that only authorized personnel can access the sensitive data.

